{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat memory/taxonomy.md'","aggregated_output":"- **Improve the solution** `T`\n  - decide: what can I change to improve my solution? → Improve the evidence for choosing changes | Repair incorrect or mismatched predictions | Change the information presented to the model | Change training examples or targets | Change the learned model | Change the prediction pool | Change prediction combination or output mapping | Make useful experiments feasible\n  - desiderata · what fits this case?: The current predictions fail a schema, signal, or compatibility check. · The claimed gain is no larger than variation in prior unchanged runs. · Errors concentrate where the existing input representation loses available information. · A known noisy or shifted subgroup dominates the training errors. · Learning curves show underfitting, overfitting, or unstable optimization despite correct inputs. · Available candidate predictors make complementary held-out errors. · Valid cached predictions exist and output mapping or blending is not yet compared on held-out data. · Resource failures prevent the intended full pipeline or meaningful trials from completing.\n  - **Improve the evidence for choosing changes** `T.1`\n    - decide: Which uncertainty should I resolve before another performance change? → Choose a validation design | Choose a trustworthy comparison | Choose a response to an unsuccessful search\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Related records, source shifts, or unstable target proportions undermine the present split. · Recent comparisons are confounded, noisy, or use a questionable metric. · Several recent trials failed to beat a reproducible baseline.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: Marginal gains from the current line of changes have diminished to a small fraction of earlier improvements, suggesting saturation. · No working pipeline, validation scheme, or baseline model exists yet, so foundational infrastructure is the first uncertainty to resolve. · The pipeline cannot produce a valid submission due to runtime errors or missing components, blocking any performance comparison.\n    - **Choose a validation design** `T.1.1`\n      - decide: Which split design should I test for this data? → Preserve target proportions | Hold related examples together | Probe subgroup allocation | Change holdout allocation\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Target classes or target bins are unevenly distributed across the current folds. · Multiple rows share an entity, source, or topic that should be unseen at evaluation. · A recognizable source subgroup differs from the evaluation population. · The proposed subgroup allocation places the same row in training and validation. · A large fixed holdout substantially reduces fitting data after model choices are settled.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The stratification variable used for fold assignment may not be appropriate for the target distribution (e.g., soft or noisy labels). · The grouping or clustering step for CV splitting receives incorrect input (non-feature columns, wrong data type, or mismatched features between fit and predict). · The number of CV folds is mismatched with the number of natural groups or the dataset size, affecting fold balance or estimate stability. · The target array passed to the stratified splitter is not in the expected discrete format (e.g., float instead of integer bins). · The column used for group assignment in the CV splitter does not correspond to the actual entity, topic, or source that should define groups. · The pipeline currently lacks any holdout or validation split, so there is no local scoring mechanism.\n      - **Preserve target proportions** `T.1.1.1`\n      - **Hold related examples together** `T.1.1.2`\n      - **Probe subgroup allocation** `T.1.1.3`\n      - **Change holdout allocation** `T.1.1.4`\n    - **Choose a trustworthy comparison** `T.1.2`\n      - decide: Which measurement would resolve the uncertainty in the apparent gain? → Measure repeat-run variability | Check local and external score agreement | Isolate one change | Align the evaluation callback | Inspect data structure\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Unchanged runs vary by as much as the proposed improvement. · The public evaluation is tiny or disagrees with local model rankings. · The compared versions changed multiple independent settings. · Early stopping monitors a proxy, wrong dataset, or wrong metric direction. · The prediction unit or ordering of raw observations is not yet established.\n      - **Measure repeat-run variability** `T.1.2.1`\n      - **Check local and external score agreement** `T.1.2.2`\n      - **Isolate one change** `T.1.2.3`\n      - **Align the evaluation callback** `T.1.2.4`\n      - **Inspect data structure** `T.1.2.5`\n    - **Choose a response to an unsuccessful search** `T.1.3`\n      - decide: Should I restore, narrow, or redirect the current experiment? → Restore the proven configuration | Move to a different improvement lever | Narrow the parameter search | Establish a working baseline | Test a coordinated bundle\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: A reproducible regression followed a change and the prior working configuration is available. · Several trials along the same lever show no reliable improvement. · Nearby tested parameter values bracket an improving region. · No complete pipeline has produced a valid measured prediction. · The candidate changes are coupled and a saved baseline permits later ablation.\n      - **Restore the proven configuration** `T.1.3.1`\n      - **Move to a different improvement lever** `T.1.3.2`\n      - **Narrow the parameter search** `T.1.3.3`\n      - **Establish a working baseline** `T.1.3.4`\n      - **Test a coordinated bundle** `T.1.3.5`\n  - **Repair incorrect or mismatched predictions** `T.2`\n    - decide: Which failure mechanism should I investigate first? → Restore model and input compatibility | Locate a data-flow defect | Resolve the first execution failure\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: A new artifact or representation was introduced immediately before the failure. · Execution completes but outputs are constant, implausible, or omit configured components. · A traceback or absent output file identifies an execution dependency failure.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: The model architecture definition does not match the structure expected by the checkpoint weights being loaded. · Inference-time hyperparameters (e.g., batch size) differ from training-time settings in a way that affects batch-dependent layers. · The ensemble blending weights do not reflect the known quality differences between component models. · The preprocessing applied during inference differs from what was used during training of the loaded checkpoint. · The config file shipped with new checkpoints contains default parameter values that differ from the actual training configuration. · The rank aggregation formula contains an arithmetic inconsistency (e.g., offset or scaling) that distorts the combined predictions.\n    - **Restore model and input compatibility** `T.2.1`\n      - decide: Which training-to-inference contract is broken? → Match architecture and weights | Match input preprocessing | Match tokenizer and vocabulary | Match feature schema and encoders\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Checkpoint keys or tensor dimensions disagree with the reconstructed model. · Inference transforms differ from those recorded for the training checkpoint. · Vocabulary, special tokens, or token positions differ from training. · A downstream model receives different columns, encodings, or dimensions from those used in fitting.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: Input file paths use a different ID format (e.g., float vs integer) than the files on disk, causing lookup failures. · The output activation or transformation (e.g., softmax vs log-softmax vs sigmoid) at inference differs from what the checkpoint was trained to produce. · The inference pipeline uses a different number of folds or ensemble members than the training setup that produced the checkpoint(s). · The inference script assumes a different training objective or output format (e.g., ordinal vs regression vs classification) than what the checkpoint was actually trained with. · The installed package or library version at inference differs from the version used to produce the checkpoint, causing incompatible layer definitions or serialization formats. · A variable in the preprocessing or inference code references the wrong or stale dataframe/array, so the intended fix or transform is not actually applied.\n      - **Match architecture and weights** `T.2.1.1`\n      - **Match input preprocessing** `T.2.1.2`\n      - **Match tokenizer and vocabulary** `T.2.1.3`\n      - **Match feature schema and encoders** `T.2.1.4`\n    - **Locate a data-flow defect** `T.2.2`\n      - decide: Where should I check for lost or corrupted signal? → Restore row and identifier alignment | Restore missing model contributions | Correct aggregation arithmetic | Trace inputs and prediction signal | Repair invalid numerical values | Correct the custom objective implementation\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Data or predictions were sorted, concatenated, or joined without an explicit identifier mapping. · Configured model or fold counts exceed the number of accumulated predictions. · The blend magnitude or shape changes unexpectedly with member count. · Model or stage outputs are constant, placeholders, or derived from the wrong dataset. · Inputs, losses, or intermediate activations contain nonfinite values. · A custom objective edit preceded divergence or a catastrophic score loss.\n      - **Restore row and identifier alignment** `T.2.2.1`\n      - **Restore missing model contributions** `T.2.2.2`\n      - **Correct aggregation arithmetic** `T.2.2.3`\n      - **Trace inputs and prediction signal** `T.2.2.4`\n      - **Repair invalid numerical values** `T.2.2.5`\n      - **Correct the custom objective implementation** `T.2.2.6`\n    - **Resolve the first execution failure** `T.2.3`\n      - decide: What dependency failure prevents the intended computation? → Resolve artifact paths | Resolve package compatibility | Match computation devices | Repair syntax or API contracts | Write the intended submission\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The error is a missing file or a read-only source path. · The failure is an import, missing symbol, or incompatible package version. · The error names an unsupported accelerator or mismatched tensor devices. · The first failure names an undefined variable, invalid keyword, type, or method. · The computation completes but the expected prediction file is absent or invalid.\n      - **Resolve artifact paths** `T.2.3.1`\n      - **Resolve package compatibility** `T.2.3.2`\n      - **Match computation devices** `T.2.3.3`\n      - **Repair syntax or API contracts** `T.2.3.4`\n      - **Write the intended submission** `T.2.3.5`\n  - **Change the information presented to the model** `T.3`\n    - decide: Which input representation should I change next? → Represent repeated or structured records | Represent text for the model | Represent images and signals | Control the feature set\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Examples contain repeated structured observations that the current features collapse inadequately. · Free text is the principal input and residuals vary with language or document structure. · Image or signal preprocessing discards potentially label-relevant detail. · An expanded or stale feature set is associated with worse validation or schema drift.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: The expert is incrementally stepping through feature-selection ratios without a systematic sweep or recorded validation scores for each step. · The evaluation metric was recently changed, requiring re-evaluation of previously tuned representation choices under the new metric. · The ongoing feature-count or representation sweep has not yet peaked — the score is still improving at the last step tried. · Numeric precision (e.g., float16 storage) causes inconsistent representations of the same value across train and test splits. · An importance-based feature ranking is available and the current top-N cutoff has room to be tuned. · Engineered interaction features were computed at the wrong granularity (row-level vs. entity-level), so they do not represent the intended signal after aggregation. · The current preprocessing code has a runtime bug or library incompatibility that prevents it from executing correctly. · Preprocessing steps (scaling, imputation, outlier removal) were already disabled or simplified for the current model family, so re-enabling them is the relevant change. · A newly assembled multimodal or multi-checkpoint pipeline has not yet been scored on the leaderboard. · The pipeline uses overlapping or redundant input windows/segments and it is unclear whether all of them add signal. · Previously removed model components or checkpoints were dropped only for compute/resource constraints, not because they hurt performance. · The expert is already mid-sweep on a text vectorizer or representation parameter, making continued tuning of that parameter the natural next step. · The feature count was recently changed drastically and needs to be co-tuned with model hyperparameters. · A heavy NLP or preprocessing dependency is causing runtime or resource failures that block submission. · Cached predictions from a deep model are available but not yet included as features in the current model's input. · New feature columns are being appended to the feature list without re-running the feature selection procedure. · Domain-specific preprocessing that was part of the best-known configuration has been removed or is not currently active. · The current feature set lacks explicit domain-derived interaction or composite features that are known to be predictive. · The pipeline applies a single imputation or preprocessing strategy to all model families, but some models require different handling. · A rich encoder (e.g., BERT) already captures signals that explicit categorical features would duplicate, making those features redundant. · Categorical metadata is available in the data but not yet encoded as features for the current model.\n    - **Represent repeated or structured records** `T.3.1`\n      - decide: Which structured feature construction should I test? → Add entity-level summary statistics | Add temporal changes or recent windows | Add domain interactions | Change numeric precision or scaling | Change missing-value handling | Add structured metadata\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: There are multiple observations per entity but the model sees only a latest row or a narrow summary. · Recent changes or temporal order are not represented by full-history summaries. · Related measurements have interpretable differences, ratios, or interactions absent from the feature set. · Stored precision, scaling, or noisy decimals are unsuitable for the learner. · Aggregations create missing values or model families differ in native NaN support. · Available metadata has plausible target signal and is absent from the model.\n      - **Add entity-level summary statistics** `T.3.1.1`\n      - **Add temporal changes or recent windows** `T.3.1.2`\n      - **Add domain interactions** `T.3.1.3`\n      - **Change numeric precision or scaling** `T.3.1.4`\n      - **Change missing-value handling** `T.3.1.5`\n      - **Add structured metadata** `T.3.1.6`\n    - **Represent text for the model** `T.3.2`\n      - decide: Which text representation should I test next? → Change text normalization | Change text input structure | Tune sparse n-gram features | Add linguistic or structural statistics | Add pretrained dense representations | Add similarity or auxiliary-task features\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Cleaning may remove meaningful punctuation, spelling, or pretrained-tokenizer cues. · Distinct text segments or known metadata are currently flattened without structure. · The sparse representation omits common n-grams or has a large rare-token tail. · Text quality or document organization is relevant but absent from current features. · Current features capture counts but lack semantic information. · Labeled neighbors or related-task labeled data are available without using evaluation labels.\n      - **Change text normalization** `T.3.2.1`\n      - **Change text input structure** `T.3.2.2`\n      - **Tune sparse n-gram features** `T.3.2.3`\n      - **Add linguistic or structural statistics** `T.3.2.4`\n      - **Add pretrained dense representations** `T.3.2.5`\n      - **Add similarity or auxiliary-task features** `T.3.2.6`\n    - **Represent images and signals** `T.3.3`\n      - decide: Which spatial or signal representation should I test? → Sweep input resolution | Change time-frequency resolution | Change the observed window | Change signal filtering or clipping | Change input normalization | Change channel layout or rendering\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Resizing removes label-relevant detail and compatible resolution checkpoints exist. · Time-frequency detail is unresolved at the current transform settings. · The current crop misses the labeled interval or includes mostly irrelevant context. · Raw signals contain known interference, extreme amplitudes, or irrelevant frequency content. · Input scale or channel statistics differ from the pretrained model expectations. · Channel layout or pseudo-image shape obscures available signal structure.\n      - **Sweep input resolution** `T.3.3.1`\n      - **Change time-frequency resolution** `T.3.3.2`\n      - **Change the observed window** `T.3.3.3`\n      - **Change signal filtering or clipping** `T.3.3.4`\n      - **Change input normalization** `T.3.3.5`\n      - **Change channel layout or rendering** `T.3.3.6`\n    - **Control the feature set** `T.3.4`\n      - decide: Which feature-set reduction or refresh should I perform? → Ablate a feature family | Estimate feature relevance | Tune the retained feature count | Recompute feature selection | Reduce dense representation dimensions\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: A newly added feature family coincided with worse held-out performance. · No stable relevance ranking exists for the current wide feature space. · A ranking exists but its retention threshold is arbitrary or stale. · Features were added or renamed after the saved selection file was generated. · Expanded embeddings raise dimensionality sharply and validation has not improved.\n      - **Ablate a feature family** `T.3.4.1`\n      - **Estimate feature relevance** `T.3.4.2`\n      - **Tune the retained feature count** `T.3.4.3`\n      - **Recompute feature selection** `T.3.4.4`\n      - **Reduce dense representation dimensions** `T.3.4.5`\n  - **Change training examples or targets** `T.4`\n    - decide: Which data or target intervention should I test? → Choose training examples | Choose a target formulation | Choose training augmentation\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Sample quality, class proportions, or source coverage differ from the intended test population. · The label definition includes censoring, ordinal levels, or group-specific ranking. · A neural model overfits and the input admits label-preserving perturbations.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: Pseudo-labeling has shown consistent improvement on other ensemble members or similar tasks. · The target construction code contains a type mismatch or silent filtering bug that causes some rows to receive no valid label. · Target-transformation hyperparameters were tuned under a previous pipeline configuration and may be stale.\n    - **Choose training examples** `T.4.1`\n      - decide: Which change to the training population is justified? → Filter or deduplicate training data | Add labeled auxiliary data | Use pseudo-labeled examples | Reweight or resample subpopulations | Interpolate training examples\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Duplicates, overlapping windows, unreliable labels, or mismatched sources are identifiable. · Additional labeled data with compatible input and label definitions exists. · Unlabeled examples and a validated teacher or pseudo-labeled checkpoint are available. · A class or test-representative subgroup is underexposed during training. · A minority group is small and within-class interpolation is meaningful in the feature space. · Previous synthetic oversampling hurt the same held-out metric.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: Previous class-weight or scale_pos_weight adjustments in one direction have not yielded clear improvements, motivating exploration in the opposite direction. · A baseline score exists from a less strict filtering strategy, enabling comparison with stricter filtering. · Stricter quality filtering substantially reduces the training set size, risking underfitting. · Multiple inclusion/exclusion variants of a specific class have been tested but not all have leaderboard scores, so the comparison is incomplete. · A dedicated pre-training or warm-up stage on auxiliary/extra data before fine-tuning on the target data has not been tried. · A previous attempt to add extra labeled data improved the held-out metric, supporting further integration of that data. · The extra-data integration adds substantial code complexity (shape alignment, label construction, merging) that increases bug risk. · The oversampling or filtering code uses incorrect variable names or selection logic that targets the wrong subgroup. · The unsupervised feature extractor is fit on the full mixed training set, biasing representations toward the majority subpopulation.\n      - **Filter or deduplicate training data** `T.4.1.1`\n      - **Add labeled auxiliary data** `T.4.1.2`\n      - **Use pseudo-labeled examples** `T.4.1.3`\n      - **Reweight or resample subpopulations** `T.4.1.4`\n      - **Interpolate training examples** `T.4.1.5`\n    - **Choose a target formulation** `T.4.2`\n      - decide: Which supervision representation should I test? → Transform censored outcomes | Construct targets within evaluation groups | Tune target centering or transformation strength | Use ordinal or classification supervision\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Targets contain event times and censoring indicators rather than fully observed outcomes. · The evaluation separately scores predefined groups with different survival or target distributions. · A scalar target transform or centering convention changed or remains untuned. · Labels are ordered discrete levels but the current model is plain regression.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: Optimized rounding thresholds for regression-to-ordinal mapping are unstable across runs, suggesting a direct classification or ordinal formulation may be more robust. · Public solutions or domain conventions suggest using survival-function-based transforms (e.g., KM or Nelson-Aalen probabilities) as regression targets instead of raw event times.\n      - **Transform censored outcomes** `T.4.2.1`\n      - **Construct targets within evaluation groups** `T.4.2.2`\n      - **Tune target centering or transformation strength** `T.4.2.3`\n      - **Use ordinal or classification supervision** `T.4.2.4`\n    - **Choose training augmentation** `T.4.3`\n      - decide: Which augmentation should I test against the unaugmented baseline? → Use mild image perturbations | Mask signal regions | Mix labeled examples\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Small image brightness or geometric changes preserve the target semantics. · The model relies on narrow time-frequency patches that may be absent at inference. · Targets can be meaningfully mixed and current training overfits individual examples.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: Previous attempts at stronger geometric or photometric augmentations degraded scores compared to the mild baseline. · The proposed augmentation variant has not yet been scored on the leaderboard.\n      - **Use mild image perturbations** `T.4.3.1`\n      - **Mask signal regions** `T.4.3.2`\n      - **Mix labeled examples** `T.4.3.3`\n  - **Change the learned model** `T.5`\n    - decide: Which model-fitting change is the most plausible next intervention? → Choose a model family or initialization | Change the prediction architecture | Tune a tree learner | Tune neural optimization or loss\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: A correctly implemented backbone or learner has plateaued across local tuning. · A minimal head or fusion rule discards useful backbone or branch information. · The active learner is a tree model with capacity or convergence problems. · The active component is neural and its optimization or loss is suspect.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: A bug or mismatch in the model definition or weight loading prevents the new component from running or functioning correctly. · The auxiliary binning head (num_bins > 1) has been tested across configurations with inconsistent or mixed results. · Top public solutions or domain knowledge indicate a different model family (e.g., 1D CNN/RNN on raw signals) is effective for this task. · A model-fitting change from a prior edit has not yet been scored, so evaluation should precede further changes. · Recent iterations have focused on backbone/architecture swaps rather than optimization or loss changes. · A different deep learning framework is available and could enable architectures not easily built in the current one. · A newer or different pretrained checkpoint version is available for the same model architecture. · A newly introduced tree or ensemble model has not yet been tuned, so an initial hyperparameter sweep is the natural next step. · The training objective (loss function) does not align with the competition's evaluation metric, creating a train-eval mismatch. · Previous experiments suggest that certain hyperparameters (e.g., dropout and learning rate) interact and should be tuned jointly.\n    - **Choose a model family or initialization** `T.5.1`\n      - decide: Which replacement model should I test as a standalone candidate? → Change pretrained backbone or capacity | Use domain-adapted pretraining | Change tree learner or boosting variant | Fit a model for a different representation\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: A compatible alternative pretrained backbone or size is available. · Available pretraining data is substantially closer to the task domain than the current initialization. · The features are tabular or fixed vectors and the current tree implementation has plateaued. · A meaningful input representation is not served well by the current architecture.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The candidate backbone could serve as a useful ensemble component alongside the current model. · Public top solutions or strong notebooks for this competition use the candidate backbone. · A previously tested variant of the candidate backbone scored lower, confirming the current choice or motivating reversion. · The candidate is a known algorithmic variant (e.g., DART, different booster type) within the same library that addresses a specific weakness of the current default. · The change is only an API-level swap within the same library, not a fundamentally different model family. · The candidate variant was already tried and failed to produce a valid submission or caused a runtime error. · The candidate variant was already tested and showed no improvement or degraded performance compared to the current default. · The raw input data modality required by the candidate architecture is accessible and loadable in the inference environment. · A newer or different checkpoint version is available for the candidate backbone with potentially better validation metrics. · Smaller variants of the same model family have already been tested, making the next-size-up variant the natural next candidate. · The current backbone size has been exhaustively tried with multiple checkpoint versions without sustained improvement. · The candidate backbone is known or expected to be architecturally superior to the current one on similar tasks. · The backbone swap code is already implemented but has not yet been scored, so a submission run is needed to get signal. · Disabling other ensemble members allows isolating the candidate model's standalone contribution. · The candidate backbone is being used without pretrained weights, so the capacity change is purely architectural. · The candidate model framework or algorithm has not yet been tried in this pipeline. · The candidate model's prior poor score may be due to a configuration mismatch (e.g., stale thresholds, wrong postprocessing) rather than inherently worse predictions. · A previous submission with the candidate backbone achieved the best single-model leaderboard score. · A larger backbone underperformed a smaller backbone, suggesting model capacity may not be the bottleneck. · The candidate model submission uses fewer ensemble members (folds/seeds) than the best prior submission, making comparison unfair. · The candidate framework offers computational advantages (e.g., GPU acceleration) that could enable faster iteration. · The candidate model's hyperparameter sweep has not yet been submitted, so the current best LB score from the existing model remains the benchmark.\n      - **Change pretrained backbone or capacity** `T.5.1.1`\n      - **Use domain-adapted pretraining** `T.5.1.2`\n      - **Change tree learner or boosting variant** `T.5.1.3`\n      - **Fit a model for a different representation** `T.5.1.4`\n    - **Change the prediction architecture** `T.5.2`\n      - decide: Which architectural component should I alter? → Change head capacity or attention | Change pooling or representation normalization | Add auxiliary supervision | Change feature-level modality fusion | Change training-time dropout\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The backbone feeds a minimal, inappropriate shared, or oversized head. · Only one hidden layer or pooling statistic is used despite variable-length or multi-layer information. · An auxiliary target or discretized version of the main target is available. · Multiple informative branches are merged by a lossy or untested rule. · The head overfits or varies markedly across seeds.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: A different pretrained checkpoint (with potentially different training settings) is available to swap into the backbone. · The current attention/spatial block variant and its capacity settings have not been fully explored or compared against prior higher-scoring variants. · Multi-sample dropout is an applicable regularization technique for the prediction head on this small noisy regression task. · The normalization layer (e.g., BatchNorm, LayerNorm) in or before the prediction head is missing, mis-wired, or unstable due to small batch sizes. · Reinitializing the last few encoder layers is a viable technique to reduce pretrained bias for small-data fine-tuning. · The pretrained checkpoint's architecture (auxiliary head, fusion rule, pooling) must match the current model definition at load time.\n      - **Change head capacity or attention** `T.5.2.1`\n      - **Change pooling or representation normalization** `T.5.2.2`\n      - **Add auxiliary supervision** `T.5.2.3`\n      - **Change feature-level modality fusion** `T.5.2.4`\n      - **Change training-time dropout** `T.5.2.5`\n    - **Tune a tree learner** `T.5.3`\n      - decide: Which tree-fitting constraint should I adjust? → Tune tree depth and leaves | Tune penalties and subsampling | Coordinate boosting rate and rounds | Change stopping patience or criterion | Reset uncertain tree parameters\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Trees appear capacity-limited or increased capacity has begun to overfit. · Training error improves while validation worsens with the current feature set. · Learning rate changed without a corresponding convergence budget. · Stopping occurs before convergence, on the wrong set, or with excessive patience. · Many manually set parameters lack isolated evidence of benefit.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The current parameter sweep has plateaued without clear improvement across recent trials. · Row and column subsampling parameters are at their defaults (no subsampling applied). · The depth/leaves sweep has not yet identified a clear optimum (scores still improving or flat at the boundary). · The most recent parameter change has not yet been submitted or validated on the leaderboard. · The single-parameter sweep has already identified a clear optimum to anchor further interaction sweeps. · The best previous score was achieved with a known configuration, providing a reference point for the current tuning direction. · Public solutions or top notebooks for this competition use substantially different hyperparameter ranges than the current configuration. · Recent regularization parameter perturbations have yielded regressions or negligible/inconsistent score changes. · The previous capacity increase (depth or leaves) caused a score regression, suggesting a revert or reduction. · The feature count was recently changed without an isolated submission to confirm its effect. · The loaded feature subset file may differ from the one that produced the best previous score.\n      - **Tune tree depth and leaves** `T.5.3.1`\n      - **Tune penalties and subsampling** `T.5.3.2`\n      - **Coordinate boosting rate and rounds** `T.5.3.3`\n      - **Change stopping patience or criterion** `T.5.3.4`\n      - **Reset uncertain tree parameters** `T.5.3.5`\n    - **Tune neural optimization or loss** `T.5.4`\n      - decide: Which training change should I test? → Tune learning rate | Change optimizer or learning-rate schedule | Change training duration or stages | Change the training objective | Make component training cross-validated\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Training is unstable or converges poorly at the current learning rate. · The training loss stalls at a schedule transition or under the current optimizer. · Training ends while validation improves or continues well beyond its best epoch. · The training loss poorly represents the ranking, distribution, or ordinal evaluation objective. · A component lacks consistent fold training or reproducible held-out predictions.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: Changing the loss function alters effective gradient magnitudes, requiring a compensating learning rate adjustment. · The response surface around the current best hyperparameter value has been partially mapped, and small perturbations near it are the natural next step. · A previously tested hyperparameter value produced the best known score and should be reverted to as a baseline before making other changes. · The previous edit changed multiple hyperparameters simultaneously, so individual effects are confounded. · A regularization hyperparameter (e.g., dropout) was changed alongside another modification and may now be mis-calibrated for the current configuration. · The neural network's key hyperparameters (architecture size, learning rate, etc.) have not been tuned from their defaults.\n      - **Tune learning rate** `T.5.4.1`\n      - **Change optimizer or learning-rate schedule** `T.5.4.2`\n      - **Change training duration or stages** `T.5.4.3`\n      - **Change the training objective** `T.5.4.4`\n      - **Make component training cross-validated** `T.5.4.5`\n  - **Change the prediction pool** `T.6`\n    - decide: How should I change which trained predictors contribute? → Choose the source of ensemble diversity | Choose ensemble membership | Select a trained artifact\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Existing members mostly share the same architecture, features, targets, or training randomness. · There are untested, weak, or recently changed members in the current pool. · Several compatible saved runs or training-stage artifacts are available.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: The fold count has not been varied alongside member selection to understand its interaction with pool composition. · Empirical evidence shows that adding more members to the pool improved the score. · A known preprocessing or architecture variant that already improved the ensemble suggests a further related variant could add diversity. · Validation loss in checkpoint filenames does not reliably predict leaderboard performance, complicating member selection. · A smaller model variant outperformed a larger variant on the leaderboard, suggesting model size is not a reliable proxy for pool contribution. · A candidate subset ensemble has not been submitted, so there is no evidence it outperforms the current full ensemble.\n    - **Choose the source of ensemble diversity** `T.6.1`\n      - decide: What kind of additional predictor should I test? → Add a different model family | Add an alternative input representation | Add an alternative supervision recipe | Average more fold or snapshot predictions | Average independently seeded models | Average checkpoint weights\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Current members share a family and their held-out errors are highly correlated. · A relevant modality, feature set, or preprocessing view is not represented. · Alternative valid target transforms or training recipes yield different held-out errors. · Compatible unused fold or snapshot predictions are already available. · Training seeds produce materially different held-out predictions. · Compatible checkpoints from one weight-space trajectory are saved.\n      - **Add a different model family** `T.6.1.1`\n      - **Add an alternative input representation** `T.6.1.2`\n      - **Add an alternative supervision recipe** `T.6.1.3`\n      - **Average more fold or snapshot predictions** `T.6.1.4`\n      - **Average independently seeded models** `T.6.1.5`\n      - **Average checkpoint weights** `T.6.1.6`\n    - **Choose ensemble membership** `T.6.2`\n      - decide: Which membership experiment should I run next? → Measure a candidate in isolation | Test one additional member | Remove a harmful or redundant member | Replace one fixed ensemble slot | Restore complementary members | Retest membership after upstream changes\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The candidate has not yet produced verified standalone predictions. · A functioning candidate has plausible complementary errors but no marginal-blend result. · A member is weak, redundant, or associated with a regression. · A new compatible checkpoint can replace a specific weaker slot. · Removing formerly useful members caused a reproducible regression. · Other model components or aggregation rules changed since the last membership test.\n      - **Measure a candidate in isolation** `T.6.2.1`\n      - **Test one additional member** `T.6.2.2`\n      - **Remove a harmful or redundant member** `T.6.2.3`\n      - **Replace one fixed ensemble slot** `T.6.2.4`\n      - **Restore complementary members** `T.6.2.5`\n      - **Retest membership after upstream changes** `T.6.2.6`\n    - **Select a trained artifact** `T.6.3`\n      - decide: Which checkpoint comparison should I perform? → Compare trained runs or folds | Compare training stages or stopping rules | Compare full-data and fold-trained weights\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Several runs or fold checkpoints have matching architecture and preprocessing. · Artifacts differ mainly by training stage or stopping criterion. · Both full-data-trained and fold-trained artifacts are available.\n      - **Compare trained runs or folds** `T.6.3.1`\n      - **Compare training stages or stopping rules** `T.6.3.2`\n      - **Compare full-data and fold-trained weights** `T.6.3.3`\n  - **Change prediction combination or output mapping** `T.7`\n    - decide: Which operation on valid predictions should I change? → Choose a combination rule | Choose how to weight predictors | Choose meta-model inputs | Map continuous scores to discrete labels | Correct output semantics or bias | Choose inference-time averaging\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Prediction scales or the aggregation domain differ across members. · The same valid prediction pool has only an arbitrary weight allocation. · A downstream learner uses base predictions or embeddings as input features. · The task requires discrete ordered outputs or labels occupy a few repeated levels. · Prediction units, probability calibration, or subgroup residual bias need correction. · The input has valid alternate views that the current inference uses only once.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: A code bug or misconfiguration (wrong mode, missing argument, wrong column reference, syntax error, or broken file path) prevents one or more ensemble members from producing correct predictions. · The blending code or saved artifacts are stale or mismatched with the current set of ensemble members. · A previously disabled or excluded model checkpoint is available and was part of a better-scoring configuration. · The pipeline mishandles multi-fold or multi-dimensional prediction arrays, causing shape or aggregation errors. · Non-deterministic execution (e.g., stochastic preprocessing, worker ordering, unseeded randomness) causes prediction outputs to vary across identical runs.\n    - **Choose a combination rule** `T.7.1`\n      - decide: Which rule should I compare against the current aggregation? → Compare rank and raw-score averaging | Compare probability and logit aggregation | Group before averaging | Fit a regularized prediction stacker | Change the meta-learner | Try a nonlinear probability blend\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The metric rewards ordering and members have different score scales. · Members expose logits and probabilities for a distribution-based metric. · Model families contribute unequal numbers of fold or seed predictions. · Aligned leakage-free out-of-fold predictions exist for a simple learned blender. · An established stacker has plateaued and its model class or penalty is untested. · Probability saturation may make arithmetic averaging overly dominated by confident members. · Only tiny or noisy calibration data are available relative to meta-model flexibility.\n      - **Compare rank and raw-score averaging** `T.7.1.1`\n      - **Compare probability and logit aggregation** `T.7.1.2`\n      - **Group before averaging** `T.7.1.3`\n      - **Fit a regularized prediction stacker** `T.7.1.4`\n      - **Change the meta-learner** `T.7.1.5`\n      - **Try a nonlinear probability blend** `T.7.1.6`\n    - **Choose how to weight predictors** `T.7.2`\n      - decide: Which weighting strategy should I use as the next comparison? → Tune nonuniform blend weights | Use a uniform-weight baseline | Allow signed blend coefficients | Compare conditional and global weights | Retune after component changes | Normalize component scales\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Members differ reliably in quality or complementary error structure. · Equal weighting matches tuned weights within measured score noise. · Prior unconstrained or signed fits suggest a component can cancel correlated error. · Component strengths differ consistently by an observable class or subgroup. · Member quality, count, augmentation, or scaling changed after weights were selected. · Component prediction scales differ substantially or contain heavy outliers.\n      - **Tune nonuniform blend weights** `T.7.2.1`\n      - **Use a uniform-weight baseline** `T.7.2.2`\n      - **Allow signed blend coefficients** `T.7.2.3`\n      - **Compare conditional and global weights** `T.7.2.4`\n      - **Retune after component changes** `T.7.2.5`\n      - **Normalize component scales** `T.7.2.6`\n    - **Choose meta-model inputs** `T.7.3`\n      - decide: Which stacking feature change should I test? → Generate valid out-of-fold meta-features | Add auxiliary stacking features | Select a smaller meta-feature set\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Training meta-features include in-sample predictions or inconsistent fold assignments. · Additional aligned auxiliary outputs provide signal not captured by existing base scores. · The meta-feature pool has grown while validation stagnates or regresses.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The meta-learner was trained on a different set of base-model features than what is being fed at inference time. · A previously tested base-model variant (e.g., LightGBM vs XGBoost) achieved higher scores and reverting to it may improve the stack. · A prior attempt to add a candidate meta-feature source (e.g., DeBERTa OOF predictions) failed to produce a valid submission. · Fold-specific or pre-trained model checkpoints are available for generating out-of-fold predictions for a candidate meta-feature source. · External artifact dependencies (pickled models, pre-computed CSVs) are causing load failures or stale/misaligned predictions. · A useful stacking signal column is being inadvertently dropped or excluded from the test-time meta-feature set.\n      - **Generate valid out-of-fold meta-features** `T.7.3.1`\n      - **Add auxiliary stacking features** `T.7.3.2`\n      - **Select a smaller meta-feature set** `T.7.3.3`\n    - **Map continuous scores to discrete labels** `T.7.4`\n      - decide: Which discretization rule should I compare? → Optimize ordinal cutpoints | Use simple clip-and-round | Match empirical target frequencies | Merge or threshold near-equal scores | Compare group-specific cutpoints | Handle degenerate bins and ties\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The ordinal metric is sensitive to cutpoints and representative held-out predictions are available. · Optimized thresholds have unstable or worse independent performance than simple rounding. · Known target frequencies are stable enough to support a distribution-matching comparison. · Continuous predictions correspond to a small set of repeated target levels. · Subgroups exhibit different residual-to-label mappings and are observable at prediction time. · Existing binning collapses a column or creates many artificial ties. · The checkpoint or ensemble changed since calibration was fitted.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The evaluation metric is rank-based (e.g., Spearman), so discretization that creates ties or destroys orderings can only degrade performance.\n      - **Optimize ordinal cutpoints** `T.7.4.1`\n      - **Use simple clip-and-round** `T.7.4.2`\n      - **Match empirical target frequencies** `T.7.4.3`\n      - **Merge or threshold near-equal scores** `T.7.4.4`\n      - **Compare group-specific cutpoints** `T.7.4.5`\n      - **Handle degenerate bins and ties** `T.7.4.6`\n    - **Correct output semantics or bias** `T.7.5`\n      - decide: Which output correction should I test or repair? → Restore prediction units and activation | Align risk-score direction | Rescale probabilities or logits | Correct systematic prediction bias | Apply auxiliary or metadata corrections | Ablate an existing output transform\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Outputs have the wrong units, activation, range, or probability sum. · Some components rank risk in the opposite direction. · Valid probabilities show systematic confidence or per-class mass bias. · A systematic additive or reference-relative bias remains in valid predictions. · A high-precision auxiliary classifier or metadata rule identifies a predictable error subset. · A previously helpful post-processing step has not been reassessed after upstream changes. · The proposed global monotone rescaling cannot change the metric's ordering.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The threshold or scope for applying the correction is too narrow (or too broad) to produce meaningful impact. · The proposed correction risks overfitting to a very small validation or test set. · The correction's strength hyperparameter (weight, boost, mask value) has not been validated or swept systematically. · The detection rule identifying which predictions to correct may itself be flawed, independent of the correction magnitude. · A control-flow bug (stray break, early return, or loop error) causes only a subset of models or corrections to be applied. · No validation or leaderboard feedback from prior correction values is available to guide the direction of the next adjustment. · The accumulated post-processing code has become too tangled with contradictory or duplicate rules to debug incrementally, warranting a clean rewrite.\n      - **Restore prediction units and activation** `T.7.5.1`\n      - **Align risk-score direction** `T.7.5.2`\n      - **Rescale probabilities or logits** `T.7.5.3`\n      - **Correct systematic prediction bias** `T.7.5.4`\n      - **Apply auxiliary or metadata corrections** `T.7.5.5`\n      - **Ablate an existing output transform** `T.7.5.6`\n    - **Choose inference-time averaging** `T.7.6`\n      - decide: Which repeated-view inference strategy should I test? → Average deterministic symmetry views | Average windows or resolutions | Compare stochastic forward-pass averaging | Disable unintended stochastic inference\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: A deterministic flip or sensor symmetry preserves the label. · Multiple valid windows or compatible resolutions cover the same prediction target. · Repeated forward passes intentionally vary and prior stochastic-view evidence is promising. · Repeated passes should be identical but differ because training-mode layers or random transforms remain active. · The proposed augmentation changes signal meaning or previously hurt this branch.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: Repeated identical-code re-runs show substantial score variance (>0.01), confirming non-determinism is the dominant source of observed score changes. · A with-TTA leaderboard score already exists, providing a valid baseline for comparing against a without-TTA submission. · Prediction entropy measurement is planned or available to decide whether stochastic conditioning on uncertainty is worthwhile. · The model ensemble or preprocessing pipeline has changed since the last TTA ablation, potentially invalidating prior TTA results. · The current edit confounds the TTA change with another simultaneous change (e.g., re-enabling a disabled branch), preventing clean attribution. · A duplicated or redundant inference block exists that could cause double-counting of predictions.\n      - **Average deterministic symmetry views** `T.7.6.1`\n      - **Average windows or resolutions** `T.7.6.2`\n      - **Compare stochastic forward-pass averaging** `T.7.6.3`\n      - **Disable unintended stochastic inference** `T.7.6.4`\n  - **Make useful experiments feasible** `T.8`\n    - decide: Which execution change removes the current bottleneck? → Control peak resource use | Shorten experiment turnaround | Make pipeline changes reproducible\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Peak memory or per-batch throughput is the limiting resource. · Repeated trials recompute unchanged work or fail late after expensive training. · Hard-coded paths, duplicated model code, or unrecorded settings cause configuration drift.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: A code edit introduced a reference error (e.g., deleting or removing a variable still used downstream). · A debug/development flag or early-exit guard is preventing the full production pipeline from executing. · Full cross-validation with multiple seeds/folds is too slow for broad hyperparameter exploration. · The chosen algorithm variant (e.g., DART boosting) is significantly slower per iteration than alternatives, making current fold/seed counts infeasible for exploration. · Verbose debug/print statements clutter output and slow iteration when reviewing results. · A per-entity or per-row loop with repeated GPU-to-CPU transfers or format conversions dominates runtime. · CPU-based preprocessing (e.g., spectrogram computation) is a significant runtime bottleneck that could be GPU-accelerated. · Numerical precision settings during inference are a plausible source of score variation or degradation. · Model constructor parameters or backbone configuration do not match the checkpoint's training configuration. · The current notebook is a training notebook that must be converted to an inference/submission pipeline before test-set experiments can run. · The inference script does not persist intermediate outputs (logits, embeddings) needed for a planned downstream experiment. · Import errors or missing dependencies are the immediate cause of pipeline failure. · A metric computation or post-processing step called repeatedly (e.g., inside CV or optimization) dominates wall-clock time.\n    - **Control peak resource use** `T.8.1`\n      - decide: Which resource intervention fits the observed bottleneck? → Stream complete entities or prediction batches | Release sequential model state | Reduce stored feature precision | Tune inference batch size | Compare inference precision | Optimize expensive computation\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The full data or feature table cannot fit, but complete entities can be processed independently. · Memory accumulates across successive model loads. · Large feature matrices use wider dtypes than their range and required precision justify. · Peak memory occurs inside one forward pass and scales with batch size. · Model activations dominate memory and reduced precision is supported by the inference stack. · A metric, transform, or repeated device transfer dominates elapsed time.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: A previous memory-reduction intervention was already applied but did not resolve the resource failure. · A variable targeted for deletion is still referenced later in the pipeline, so removing it would cause an error or require recomputation. · Only a subset of columns is needed from the loaded file, and reading all columns unnecessarily inflates peak memory. · Intermediate DataFrames or arrays remain live in memory after they have been consumed (merged, aggregated, or copied), accumulating unnecessary RAM usage. · A single operation produces a large wide intermediate result that can be split into sequential smaller operations without changing the final output. · The GPU implementation of a computation has failed repeatedly and a working CPU fallback is available. · The observed failure is caused by a model configuration or architecture mismatch rather than a genuine resource bottleneck. · Python del and gc.collect() alone are insufficient to free GPU memory without an explicit framework cache-clearing call (e.g., torch.cuda.empty_cache()).\n      - **Stream complete entities or prediction batches** `T.8.1.1`\n      - **Release sequential model state** `T.8.1.2`\n      - **Reduce stored feature precision** `T.8.1.3`\n      - **Tune inference batch size** `T.8.1.4`\n      - **Compare inference precision** `T.8.1.5`\n      - **Optimize expensive computation** `T.8.1.6`\n    - **Shorten experiment turnaround** `T.8.2`\n      - decide: What can I reduce or reuse without invalidating the experiment? → Run a reduced end-to-end smoke test | Cache expensive reusable artifacts | Resume only unfinished training | Restore full production execution\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The current task is checking correctness and failures occur after expensive work. · Only downstream settings change while upstream features or predictions remain identical. · Some folds or training stages are complete but session limits prevent finishing the rest. · The next evaluated run must use the complete selected configuration but debug shortcuts remain enabled.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The hyperparameter sweep has converged on a candidate value, so the next run should use the full/production configuration. · The expert is iterating over a single hyperparameter and needs fast per-trial feedback rather than full production runs. · The reduced-capacity smoke-test settings (e.g., low n_estimators) may be too aggressive, preventing early stopping or other mechanisms from finding a meaningful optimum and giving misleading directional signal. · Reducing the number of folds (e.g., from 10 to 5) still produces a valid end-to-end submission while substantially cutting training time. · A previous reduced-capacity smoke test completed the full pipeline successfully, confirming the pipeline is end-to-end functional at lower settings. · The full dataset is too large for fast iteration on EDA or feature engineering, so a smaller subset should be used. · A cleaned/filtered data subset can be persisted to disk to avoid re-running the full load-and-filter pipeline each iteration. · A smaller or lighter model variant completes training/inference faster, enabling quicker iteration on the pipeline. · A long-running operation lacks progress reporting, making it impossible to distinguish a hang from slow execution. · The public test set is very small, so fewer folds or a single fold suffices for generating valid predictions. · An environment-detectable signal (e.g., sample_submission row count) can automatically distinguish debug from production mode, enabling automatic shortcut toggling.\n      - **Run a reduced end-to-end smoke test** `T.8.2.1`\n      - **Cache expensive reusable artifacts** `T.8.2.2`\n      - **Resume only unfinished training** `T.8.2.3`\n      - **Restore full production execution** `T.8.2.4`\n    - **Make pipeline changes reproducible** `T.8.3`\n      - decide: Which code-organization change addresses the observed fragility? → Parameterize and separate model pipelines | Verify a cleanup or inference-only refactor | Pin configurations and artifacts | Make missing-component behavior explicit\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Adding model variants repeatedly requires editing duplicated code and shared globals. · Dead code or training-only paths obscure a stable inference pipeline. · Identical code can load different artifacts or settings across runs. · Some component files are missing or current exception handlers silently inject placeholders.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: A code-organization change has not yet been verified to produce a complete end-to-end submission. · Scores from repeated identical runs have not yet been compared to confirm the pipeline produces stable, reproducible results. · Upstream artifacts (checkpoints, datasets) may be refreshed between runs, causing score variation unrelated to code changes. · The edit mixes functional changes with non-functional cleanup, preventing isolation of which change caused any observed effect. · A known-good baseline score from the pre-refactor pipeline exists to serve as a parity target for the new pipeline. · Systematic variation of pipeline parameters (fold count, backbone size) is needed to isolate whether a score gap is due to single-component variance or a systematic pipeline bug.\n      - **Parameterize and separate model pipelines** `T.8.3.1`\n      - **Verify a cleanup or inference-only refactor** `T.8.3.2`\n      - **Pin configurations and artifacts** `T.8.3.3`\n      - **Make missing-component behavior explicit** `T.8.3.4`","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
