uest\nenough when:  top option >= 58% (lower quartile over 117 visits) or >= 38 points ahead; median 2 checks\ncome back:    after '4+ scored steps without a new best' (4/7); after 'regression after a single change' (1/7); after 'after a new best' (1/7)  [7 returns, 1 improved the score]\n\nscores: LLM-guessed; top option matches the option taken in 85% of visits# [Choose a response to an unsuccessful search › Should I restore, narrow, or redirect the current experiment?]   311 visits, 43 experts\n\noptions: Restore the proven configuration (38) | Move to a different improvement lever (43) | Narrow the parameter search (4) | Establish a working baseline (11) | Test a coordinated bundle (4)\n\ncheck first: E22 A reproducible regression followed a change and the prior working configuration is available.  imp 0.72 → +Restore the proven configuration  [first in 143/250 visits that checked it]\nthen:        E23 Several trials along the same lever show no reliable improvement.  imp 0.88 → +Move to a different improvement lever  [first in 137/282 visits that checked it]\nthen:        E25 No complete pipeline has produced a valid measured prediction.  imp 0.78 → +Establish a working baseline  [first in 25/310 visits that checked it]\nmore:         2 further checks (E26, E24), load on request\nenough when:  top option >= 34% (lower quartile over 311 visits) or >= 5 points ahead; median 4 checks\ncome back:    after 'after a new best' (6/23); after 'other' (5/23); after '4+ scored steps without a new best' (4/23)  [23 returns, 6 improved the score]\n\nscores: LLM-guessed; top option matches the option taken in 72% of visits      - decide: Which checkpoint comparison should I perform? → Compare trained runs or folds | Compare training stages or stopping rules | Compare full-data and fold-trained weights\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Several runs or fold checkpoints have matching architecture and preprocessing. · Artifacts differ mainly by training stage or stopping criterion. · Both full-data-trained and fold-trained artifacts are available.\n      - **Compare trained runs or folds** `T.6.3.1`\n      - **Compare training stages or stopping rules** `T.6.3.2`\n      - **Compare full-data and fold-trained weights** `T.6.3.3`\n  - **Change prediction combination or output mapping** `T.7`\n    - decide: Which operation on valid predictions should I change? → Choose a combination rule | Choose how to weight predictors | Choose meta-model inputs | Map continuous scores to discrete labels | Correct output semantics or bias | Choose inference-time averaging\n    - desiderata · what fits this case?: Prediction scales or the aggregation domain differ across members. · The same valid prediction pool has only an arbitrary weight allocation. · A downstream learner uses base predictions or embeddings as input features. · The task requires discrete ordered outputs or labels occupy a few repeated levels. · Prediction units, probability calibration, or subgroup residual bias need correction. · The input has valid alternate views that the current inference uses only once.\n    - desiderata · added from expert visits: A code bug or misconfiguration (wrong mode, missing argument, wrong column reference, syntax error, or broken file path) prevents one or more ensemble members from producing correct predictions. · The blending code or saved artifacts are stale or mismatched with the current set of ensemble members. · A previously disabled or excluded model checkpoint is available and was part of a better-scoring configuration. · The pipeline mishandles multi-fold or multi-dimensional prediction arrays, causing shape or aggregation errors. · Non-deterministic execution (e.g., stochastic preprocessing, worker ordering, unseeded randomness) causes prediction outputs to vary across identical runs.\n    - **Choose a combination rule** `T.7.1`\n      - decide: Which rule should I compare against the current aggregation? → Compare rank and raw-score averaging | Compare probability and logit aggregation | Group before averaging | Fit a regularized prediction stacker | Change the meta-learner | Try a nonlinear probability blend\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The metric rewards ordering and members have different score scales. · Members expose logits and probabilities for a distribution-based metric. · Model families contribute unequal numbers of fold or seed predictions. · Aligned leakage-free out-of-fold predictions exist for a simple learned blender. · An established stacker has plateaued and its model class or penalty is untested. · Probability saturation may make arithmetic averaging overly dominated by confident members. · Only tiny or noisy calibration data are available relative to meta-model flexibility.\n      - **Compare rank and raw-score averaging** `T.7.1.1`\n      - **Compare probability and logit aggregation** `T.7.1.2`\n      - **Group before averaging** `T.7.1.3`\n      - **Fit a regularized prediction stacker** `T.7.1.4`\n      - **Change the meta-learner** `T.7.1.5`\n      - **Try a nonlinear probability blend** `T.7.1.6`\n    - **Choose how to weight predictors** `T.7.2`\n      - decide: Which weighting strategy should I use as the next comparison? → Tune nonuniform blend weights | Use a uniform-weight baseline | Allow signed blend coefficients | Compare conditional and global weights | Retune after component changes | Normalize component scales\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Members differ reliably in quality or complementary error structure. · Equal weighting matches tuned weights within measured score noise. · Prior unconstrained or signed fits suggest a component can cancel correlated error. · Component strengths differ consistently by an observable class or subgroup. · Member quality, count, augmentation, or scaling changed after weights were selected. · Component prediction scales differ substantially or contain heavy outliers.\n      - **Tune nonuniform blend weights** `T.7.2.1`\n      - **Use a uniform-weight baseline** `T.7.2.2`\n      - **Allow signed blend coefficients** `T.7.2.3`\n      - **Compare conditional and global weights** `T.7.2.4`\n      - **Retune after component changes** `T.7.2.5`\n      - **Normalize component scales** `T.7.2.6`\n    - **Choose meta-model inputs** `T.7.3`\n      - decide: Which stacking feature change should I test? → Generate valid out-of-fold meta-features | Add auxiliary stacking features | Select a smaller meta-feature set\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Training meta-features include in-sample predictions or inconsistent fold assignments. · Additional aligned auxiliary outputs provide signal not captured by existing base scores. · The meta-feature pool has grown while validation stagnates or regresses.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The meta-learner was trained on a different set of base-model features than what is being fed at inference time. · A previously tested base-model variant (e.g., LightGBM vs XGBoost) achieved higher scores and reverting to it may improve the stack. · A prior attempt to add a candidate meta-feature source (e.g., DeBERTa OOF predictions) failed to produce a valid submission. · Fold-specific or pre-trained model checkpoints are available for generating out-of-fold predictions for a candidate meta-feature source. · External artifact dependencies (pickled models, pre-computed CSVs) are causing load failures or stale/misaligned predictions. · A useful stacking signal column is being inadvertently dropped or excluded from the test-time meta-feature set.\n      - **Generate valid out-of-fold meta-features** `T.7.3.1`\n      - **Add auxiliary stacking features** `T.7.3.2`\n      - **Select a smaller meta-feature set** `T.7.3.3`\n    - **Map continuous scores to discrete labels** `T.7.4`\n      - decide: Which discretization rule should I compare? → Optimize ordinal cutpoints | Use simple clip-and-round | Match empirical target frequencies | Merge or threshold near-equal scores | Compare group-specific cutpoints | Handle degenerate bins and ties\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: The ordinal metric is sensitive to cutpoints and representative held-out predictions are available. · Optimized thresholds have unstable or worse independent performance than simple rounding. · Known target frequencies are stable enough to support a distribution-matching comparison. · Continuous predictions correspond to a small set of repeated target levels. · Subgroups exhibit different residual-to-label mappings and are observable at prediction time. · Existing binning collapses a column or creates many artificial ties. · The checkpoint or ensemble changed since calibration was fitted.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The evaluation metric is rank-based (e.g., Spearman), so discretization that creates ties or destroys orderings can only degrade performance.\n      - **Optimize ordinal cutpoints** `T.7.4.1`\n      - **Use simple clip-and-round** `T.7.4.2`\n      - **Match empirical target frequencies** `T.7.4.3`\n      - **Merge or threshold near-equal scores** `T.7.4.4`\n      - **Compare group-specific cutpoints** `T.7.4.5`\n      - **Handle degenerate bins and ties** `T.7.4.6`\n    - **Correct output semantics or bias** `T.7.5`\n      - decide: Which output correction should I test or repair? → Restore prediction units and activation | Align risk-score direction | Rescale probabilities or logits | Correct systematic prediction bias | Apply auxiliary or metadata corrections | Ablate an existing output transform\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: Outputs have the wrong units, activation, range, or probability sum. · Some components rank risk in the opposite direction. · Valid probabilities show systematic confidence or per-class mass bias. · A systematic additive or reference-relative bias remains in valid predictions. · A high-precision auxiliary classifier or metadata rule identifies a predictable error subset. · A previously helpful post-processing step has not been reassessed after upstream changes. · The proposed global monotone rescaling cannot change the metric's ordering.\n      - desiderata · added from expert visits: The threshold or scope for applying the correction is too narrow (or too broad) to produce meaningful impact. · The proposed correction risks overfitting to a very small validation or test set. · The correction's strength hyperparameter (weight, boost, mask value) has not been validated or swept systematically. · The detection rule identifying which predictions to correct may itself be flawed, independent of the correction magnitude. · A control-flow bug (stray break, early return, or loop error) causes only a subset of models or corrections to be applied. · No validation or leaderboard feedback from prior correction values is available to guide the direction of the next adjustment. · The accumulated post-processing code has become too tangled with contradictory or duplicate rules to debug incrementally, warranting a clean rewrite.\n      - **Restore prediction units and activation** `T.7.5.1`\n      - **Align risk-score direction** `T.7.5.2`\n      - **Rescale probabilities or logits** `T.7.5.3`\n      - **Correct systematic prediction bias** `T.7.5.4`\n      - **Apply auxiliary or metadata corrections** `T.7.5.5`\n      - **Ablate an existing output transform** `T.7.5.6`\n    - **Choose inference-time averaging** `T.7.6`\n      - decide: Which repeated-view inference strategy should I test? → Average deterministic symmetry views | Average windows or resolutions | Compare stochastic forward-pass averaging | Disable unintended stochastic inference\n      - desiderata · what fits this case?: A deterministic flip or sensor symmetry preserves the label. · Multiple valid windows or compatible resolutions cover the same prediction target. · Repeated forward passes intentionally vary and prior stochastic-view evidence is promising. 